  {/* ==================== 5. do not sell or share ==================== */}
  <section class="section" id="do-not-sell">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="section-sub">
      The California opt-out, and exactly what it does here.
    </p>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        <strong>Bleepboard does not sell personal information for money, and never has.</strong>
        California law also covers “sharing”, which is a wider idea: handing identifiers to
        advertising partners for cross-context behavioural advertising counts, even when no
        money changes hands and even when the collection happens through the partner’s own
        code rather than ours.
      </p>
      {!ADS_LIVE && (
        <p>
          With no ad network switched on, nothing is being shared in that sense right now.
          There is no advertising identifier to withdraw and no profile to switch off. The
          routes below are the ones that will apply when advertising is added, and the
          browser-level ones are worth setting now regardless, since they cover every site
          rather than only this one.
        </p>
      )}
      {ADS_LIVE && (
        <p>
          Advertising does run here, so ad partners may collect identifiers through their
          own technology. That is treated as “sharing”, and you have the right to opt out.
          Here is how, in the order that actually works.
        </p>
      )}
    </div>

    <div class="dns">
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <b>Your consent answer</b>
          {CMP_LIVE && (
            <>
              <span>
                Reopen the consent tool and change what you allow. This is the control that
                governs the tags on this site directly.
              </span>
              <button class="dns-btn" id="cmp-reopen" type="button">
                Change my consent choices
              </button>
              <span class="dns-note" id="cmp-reopen-note" hidden></span>
            </>
          )}
          {MEDIAVINE_LIVE && (
            <span>
              The consent tool that came with the ad script carries its own privacy settings
              control, and reopening it lets you change what you allow. Clearing cookies and
              site data for bleepboard.com also erases the stored answer, so the banner asks
              again on your next visit.
            </span>
          )}
          {!CONSENT_TOOL && (
            <span>
              No consent tool is loaded on the site at the moment, so there is nothing here
              to reopen. When one is running, this step becomes a button that reopens it,
              and this sentence goes.
            </span>
          )}
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>The advertising opt-outs</b>
          <span>
            <a href="#opt-outs">The opt-out routes above</a> switch off personalised
            advertising at the vendors themselves, which is the only place it can actually
            be switched off. Google Ads Settings covers Google’s own ad technology; the
            aboutads.info, YourAdChoices and Your Online Choices pages each cover a slice of
            the wider industry.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Global Privacy Control</b>
          <span>
            Some browsers and extensions send a Global Privacy Control signal, which is a
            legally recognised opt-out in California. Vendors that support it read it
            directly from your browser. We neither add the signal nor strip it, and since
            this site holds no profile of its own there is nothing here for it to switch
            off.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Clear the site’s storage</b>
          <span>
            Clearing cookies and site data for bleepboard.com removes any consent record and
            any advertising cookie stored for this domain, along with your favourites list.
            It is blunt, it works, and it is entirely under your control.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Ask us</b>
          <span>
            Email <b class="mail">{PRIVACY_TO}</b> and we will answer. Be aware of the
            honest limit: we hold no account, no profile and no record keyed to you, so
            there is nothing on our side to look up or delete. What we can do is confirm
            what runs and point you at the party that holds the identifier.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <p class="origin-note">
      <b>Note</b>
      <span>
        We cannot delete a cookie set by another company, and no button on this page can.
        Anything on this site that claims to opt you out either calls the consent tool that
