    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The catalogue here is Roblox, Five Nights at Freddy’s, SpongeBob and Minecraft
        audio, so a good share of the people playing it are children. Bleepboard is a
        general-audience site rather than one directed at children, but we are under no
        illusions about who plays a soundboard.
      </p>
      <p>
        There are no accounts, no sign-up, no profiles, no comments, no uploads and no
        forms. There is nowhere on this site for a child, or for anyone, to enter a name, an
        age, an email address or a photograph, because no such field exists. We do not
        knowingly collect personal information from children. If you believe a child has
        somehow provided personal information to us, contact us and we will delete it.
        Realistically the only route is an email sent to one of our addresses, and an email
        we are asked to delete gets deleted.
      </p>

      <h3>Child-directed treatment</h3>
      {CHILD_DIRECTED && (
        <p>
          The site is tagged for child-directed treatment in its ad configuration. Ad
          requests carry that flag and every ad unit is marked with it, which tells the ad
          network to serve non-personalised ads and to apply its own child-directed rules.
          It lowers what the ads earn. Given the audience, that is the right trade.
        </p>
      )}
      {!CHILD_DIRECTED && !ADS_LIVE && (
        <p>
          Ad networks offer a child-directed setting that turns off personalised advertising
          for a whole site. Bleepboard is set to standard treatment rather than
          child-directed. That has no practical effect today, because no ad request is made
          at all, and it becomes live the moment an ad network is switched on. If the
          setting changes, this paragraph changes with it, because both read from the same
          line of configuration.
        </p>
      )}
      {!CHILD_DIRECTED && ADS_LIVE && (
        <p>
          Ad requests from this site are currently sent under standard treatment rather than
          child-directed treatment, which means personalised advertising is permitted where
          consent allows it.
        </p>
