    </p>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Bleepboard is a static site hosted on GitHub Pages. Every page is generated ahead of
        time and published as a plain file. There is no application server, no database, and
        nothing that could hold a user record even if we wanted one.
      </p>
      <p>
        Like every web host, GitHub receives the requests your browser makes and keeps
        standard access logs. These typically include your IP address, the page requested,
        the time, and your browser’s user-agent string. This is how the web has worked since
        the beginning: a server cannot send you a page without knowing where to send it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those logs belong to GitHub and are handled under GitHub’s privacy statement. We do
        not have access to them, we cannot query them, and we do not receive any report
        derived from them. That also means we cannot look up, export or delete a log entry
        on request. The honest answer is to point you at the host rather than to imply a
        control we do not have.
      </p>
